        "heading": "2 RELATED WORK",
        "text": "Temporal Action Segmentation aims to segment an untrimmed video and label each segment with one of a set of pre-defined action labels. Some network-based methods capture both short- and long-term dependencies Farha & Gall (2019); Gao et al. (2021). Some methods used are weaklysupervised Li et al. (2021); Fayyaz & Gall (2020). Others propose unsupervised segmentation of complex activities without any additional input Sarfraz et al. (2021); Li & Todorovic (2021).\nTemporal Action Proposal Generation. Unlike temporal action segmentation, which refers to both action localization and recognition of actions from a given set, temporal action proposal generation (TAPG) is about more general localization of actions without knowledge of the names, number, and order of the actions, which requires long-range global information. Wang et al. (2021) captures both frame-level and proposal-level dependencies by using boundary and proposal Transformers in parallel. Tan et al. (2021) presents a Transformer based framework for direct action proposal generation by capturing global inter-proposal relationships. Xu et al. (2020); Chen et al. (2021) formulate the action detection problem as a sub-graph localization problem using a graph convolutional network (GCN). By providing more flexible and precise action proposals, TAPG can help to correct the results of the temporal action segmentation or action recognition and provide a foundation for other applications.\nTransformer Based Methods. TAPGT Wang et al. (2021) uses Transformer for proposal generation, by weighing the input of the proposal Transformer by pre-calculated boundary scores. RTD-Net Tan et al. (2021) proposes to adopt two Transformers to generate the boundary and proposal in parallel. Our MatNet integrates the boundary-attention strategy from Wang et al. (2021) and the boundary and proposal Transformers from Tan et al. (2021), by directly multiplying the output of the boundary Transformers \u03a6b with input 